  "live_records": {
    "q-64967": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64967",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi $ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\pi ,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\pi ,1) & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
