    "q-132599": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132599",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it.",
