    "q-36371": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36371",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
